    "q-148028": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148028",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=3.2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=3.8,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$",
      "worked_solution": "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(3)(0.25)+(2)(5)(0.15)+(4)(3)(0.35)+(4)(5)(0.25) \\\\  & =12.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =12.2-(3.2)(3.8) \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
